--TEST--
Detection is not a launderer: `Core\Mime::detect` accepts tainted octets and answers a plain case, and the octets it read are still tainted afterwards — `rule:security/launderers-are-sink-named`, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// An upload, as the octets a request hands a program: a PNG header in front of
// whatever the sender put behind it.
tainted bytes $upload = Core\Encoding::fromHex("89504e470d0a1a0a") as tainted bytes;

// Accepted, and the answer carries nothing of the argument. `detect`'s
// parameter is neutral rather than contagious because a case is not made of
// the subject's bytes, so this binding is a plain one and so is its spelling.
Core\Mime\Type $type = Core\Mime::detect($upload);
string $name = Core\Mime::mediaType($type);
echo $name, "\n";

// Refused, and this is the whole rule. A signature says what the first octets
// look like and nothing about what the rest is safe for — a PNG header sits in
// front of anything at all — so a member that had laundered here would be one
// reachable by writing `detect` before the sink. No runtime assertion can show
// it, because a plain value is assignable into a `tainted` target: the refusal
// is the pin, and it is the binding that has to fail.
bytes $stored = $upload;
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `bytes`, found `tainted bytes`
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
